 Ledger Snapshot: Goal was to check this repo for Huntarr-like auth bypass and secret exposure issues. Now: the backend has no application-level authentication at
  all. Next: treat the API as unsafe on any shared network and add a real auth boundary before exposing it beyond localhost.

  Findings

  - Critical: the backend exposes all API routes without any auth middleware, binds to 0.0.0.0, and allows any origin via CORS. An unauthenticated network client
    can call the full API surface directly. See backend/src/index.ts:16 and backend/src/index.ts:37.
  - Critical: unauthenticated callers can open SSH sessions to stored hosts and get a reusable sessionId, or directly execute arbitrary commands on those hosts via
    POST /api/terminal/execute. That is remote command execution against managed infrastructure with no login. See backend/src/routes/terminal.ts:15 and backend/
    src/routes/terminal.ts:109.
  - Critical: host SSH passwords are stored in plaintext in SQLite and are returned back in API responses on create, update, and delete because .returning() sends
    the whole row. See backend/src/db/schema.ts:4, backend/src/routes/hosts.ts:65, backend/src/routes/hosts.ts:115, and backend/src/routes/hosts.ts:199.
  - High: unauthenticated callers can rewrite infrastructure config and media config, similar to Huntarr’s unauthenticated settings write. Host records can be
    created/modified/deleted, and media service endpoints can be added/updated/deleted without auth. See backend/src/routes/hosts.ts:54, backend/src/routes/
    hosts.ts:89, backend/src/routes/hosts.ts:189, and backend/src/routes/media.ts:136.
  - High: unauthenticated callers can use the server as an authenticated proxy into Sonarr/Radarr/Jellyfin/Jellyseerr/Prowlarr/qBittorrent using stored API keys or
  - High: once an attacker gets or creates a sessionId, they can read, write, upload, delete, rename, archive, unarchive, and stream files on the remote host. That
    is not a Huntarr-style local config leak; it is direct remote filesystem control. See backend/src/routes/files.ts:86, backend/src/routes/files.ts:129, and
    backend/src/routes/files.ts:214.

  Mapping To Huntarr

  - Yes, it has the same core class of issue as Huntarr T1/T5: unauthenticated state-changing endpoints and authenticated downstream access without a login
    boundary.
  - It does not appear to have direct equivalents of Huntarr’s setup_mode, 2FA, recovery-key, or setup-clear issues, because this app has no setup/auth system to
    bypass.
  - I did not find a Huntarr-style ZIP upload extract-all bug in local server code. The archive handling here is different, but still dangerous because it shells
    out with unsanitized input.
